    {
      "id": "morgan-stanley-020",
      "topic": "M&A",
      "question": "A company is acquired for a 50% premium and its EV/EBITDA multiple rises from 10x to 15x. What can you say about the company?",
      "answer": "If a company is acquired for a 50% premium, this means the equity value, not inherently the enterprise value, has risen by 50%. However, if EV/EBITDA also rises by 50%, as is the case here, then EV must increase by 50% as well. EV = Equity Value + Net Debt, therefore Net Debt must be $0.",
      "source": [
        "scale_techs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "morgan-stanley-021",
      "topic": "LBO",
      "question": "What are 3 reasons why a DCF would have a higher valuation than an LBO?",
      "answer": "1. In an LBO, most of the cash flows from the projection period are used to repay debt, and therefore aren’t available to accrue to the valuation\n2. Cost of Equity for an LBO is higher because the sponsor expects greater returns due to high leverage and illiquidity\n3. The higher amount of leverage and higher interest expense therefore limits both organic value creation and growth projects, producing a lower valuation",
      "source": [
        "scale_techs"
      ]
